{
  "ID": "3.3",
  "Context/Background": "To find novel treatments and drug combinations that could effectively target colorectal cancer cells (CRCs) and lower the probability of disease relapse, we designed multiple different combinations between cisplatin, cannabidiol (CBD), and intermittent serum starvation (ISS) on colorectal cancer cell lines. We analyzed differentially expressed genes and affected pathways in colorectal cancer cell lines to understand further the potential molecular mechanisms behind the treatments and their interactions.",
  "Prompt": "Based on the results of the differential expression analysis of ISS vs DMSO in colorectal cancer cells, run a pathway overrepresentation analysis for statistically significant genes (up and down combined) against the Reactome database. Then, provide a discussion of any relevant affected pathways, highlighting important up and down regulated genes in each.  Focus on any mechanisms by which intermittent serum starvation may alter cancer related pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-14 16:22:44",
  "elapsed_seconds": 206.51,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "I. Pathway‐Overrepresentation Analysis (Reactome)  \nUsing all DE genes with adjusted p < 0.05 (n = 6,555) in the ISS vs DMSO contrast, over‐representation against Reactome_Pathways_2024 returned 20 pathways at FDR < 0.05. The most significant clusters fall into three functional modules:\n\n 1. Lipid‐Metabolism (SREBP‐Driven Cholesterol Biosynthesis)  \n    • Regulation of Cholesterol Biosynthesis by SREBP (FDR = 6.7 × 10⁻⁹; 42/56 genes)  \n      Leading genes (up-regulated): SREBF1, SREBF2, HMGCR, HMGCS1, FASN, SQLE  \n    • Activation of Gene Expression by SREBF (FDR = 2.1 × 10⁻⁵; 30/43 genes)  \n      Leading genes (up-regulated): SREBF1, SREBF2, MVK, FDPS, FDFT1, MVD  \n\n 2. Secretory‐Pathway & Membrane Trafficking  \n    • Membrane Trafficking (FDR = 4.3 × 10⁻¹⁵; 302/633 genes)  \n      Core genes: SAR1B, SEC23A, SEC24A/C/D, COPA, COPB1, GBF1  \n    • ER→Golgi Anterograde Transport (FDR = 2.9 × 10⁻⁸; 87/155 genes)  \n      Core genes: SAR1B, SEC31A/B, ARF1, ARF3  \n    • Intra-Golgi & Retrograde Golgi→ER Traffic (FDR = 9.9 × 10⁻⁹; 109/204)  \n\n 3. Stress–Cell‐Cycle Control (p53 Pathway)  \n    • TP53 Regulates Transcription of Additional Cell-Cycle Genes (FDR = 1.9 × 10⁻⁴; 17/21)  \n      Leading genes (down-regulated): CDC25C, PLK2, PLK3, CNOT family members  \n    • TP53 Regulates Transcription of Genes Involved in G2 Cell-Cycle Arrest (FDR = 1.9 × 10⁻³; 14/18)  \n      Key genes: GADD45A, BAX, CCNB1, TFDP1/2  \n\nII. Mechanistic Discussion  \n1. Compensatory Lipid Synthesis: ISS strongly enriches SREBP‐driven cholesterol‐biosynthesis (HMGCR, FASN, SQLE up ~1.5–5 log₂FC). Nutrient deprivation likely activates SREBF1/2 to maintain membrane‐lipid pools for survival and proliferation.  \n2. Secretory Trafficking Remodeling: Multiple COPII/COPI components (SAR1B, SEC23A, SEC24A/C/D) are significant—suggesting ISS slows ER-to‐Golgi flux, possibly conserving energy and reducing protein‐folding load under stress.  \n3. p53-Mediated Cell-Cycle Arrest: Down‐regulation of mitotic kinases (PLK2, PLK3) and CDC25C reflects engagement of p53‐driven checkpoints, consistent with serum‐starvation‐induced quiescence.  \n\nIII. Key Up/Down-Regulated Genes in Major Modules  \n\n| Gene    | Direction | Role                              | Pathway(s)                               |\n|---------|-----------|------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| SREBF1  | Up        | Master TF of lipid synthesis       | Regulation of Cholesterol Biosynthesis  |\n| HMGCR   | Up        | Rate‐limiting cholesterol enzyme   | Cholesterol Biosynthesis                |\n| FASN    | Up        | Fatty acid synthase                | SREBF Activation                        |\n| SAR1B   | Down      | COPII vesicle‐budding GTPase       | Membrane Trafficking; ER→Golgi Transport|\n| SEC23A  | Down      | COPII coat component               | Membrane Trafficking                    |\n| CDC25C  | Down      | Cell‐cycle G2/M phosphatase        | p53 Transcription of G2 Arrest Genes    |\n| PLK3    | Down      | Stress‐responsive mitotic kinase   | p53 Transcription of Additional Genes   |\n| TP53    | Up        | Tumor suppressor TF                | p53-Regulated Transcription             |\n\nIV. Implications for CRC Therapy  \nIntermittent serum starvation reprograms CRC cells towards de novo lipid synthesis while dampening secretory capacity and enforcing p53 checkpoints. Targeting SREBP (e.g., with Betulin) or blocking compensatory cholesterol flux could synergize with starvation‐induced vulnerability. Likewise, perturbing COPII trafficking might exacerbate ER stress and sensitize cells to chemotherapeutics.\n\nV. Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. Biochemically validate SREBP activation (nuclear SREBF1/2) and measure cellular cholesterol levels under ISS.  \n2. Assess whether pharmacologic SREBP inhibition further reduces CRC viability in ISS.  \n3. Examine ER-stress markers (e.g., CHOP, BiP) to link trafficking changes to unfolded‐protein response.  \n4. Clarify whether p53‐dependent arrest is reversible upon serum refeeding or if irreversible senescence pathways are engaged.",
